00:02:31.180 --> 00:02:34.300
So what are the needs
of the warehouse today?

00:02:34.300 --> 00:02:36.720
And as an IoT developer
that's been working

00:02:36.720 --> 00:02:38.980
on a lot of different
connected products,

00:02:38.980 --> 00:02:41.830
working with a lot of
different IoT cloud solutions,

00:02:41.830 --> 00:02:44.310
the first thing that strikes
me is the sheer amount

00:02:44.310 --> 00:02:46.350
of data available.

00:02:46.350 --> 00:02:49.650
So there's products,
there's product locations,

00:02:49.650 --> 00:02:51.330
there's LIDAR points.

00:02:51.330 --> 00:02:54.990
There's all sorts of things
that without the technology

00:02:54.990 --> 00:02:56.820
you have, and now
adding robots you

00:02:56.820 --> 00:02:59.790
have things like
batteries, robots statuses,

00:02:59.790 --> 00:03:01.830
many, many, many
different types of data.

00:03:01.830 --> 00:03:05.220
And as a person that's
managing a warehouse,

00:03:05.220 --> 00:03:08.110
I want to know that there's
a way of obtaining this data,

00:03:08.110 --> 00:03:11.970
there's a way of reacting
to it, formatting it.

00:03:11.970 --> 00:03:15.400
And Google Cloud
has you covered.

00:03:15.400 --> 00:03:17.430
If you're able to
connect your Edge devices

00:03:17.430 --> 00:03:20.070
and your sensors
to Cloud IoT Core,

00:03:20.070 --> 00:03:22.680
you can quickly,
securely, and easily

00:03:22.680 --> 00:03:26.070
get that data to the cloud.

00:03:26.070 --> 00:03:29.440
From IoT Core, you get
your data into a Pub/Sub.

00:03:29.440 --> 00:03:32.910
Pub/Sub can act as a
kind of central point

00:03:32.910 --> 00:03:34.170
in your cloud architecture.

00:03:34.170 --> 00:03:37.590
From there you'd be able
to trigger cloud functions

00:03:37.590 --> 00:03:41.370
to react to your data
or data flows, Dataproc,

00:03:41.370 --> 00:03:45.690
to be able to transform your
data into an optimal format,

00:03:45.690 --> 00:03:48.480
and then ultimately
sync it to BigQuery.

00:03:48.480 --> 00:03:49.770
And I say BigQuery.

00:03:49.770 --> 00:03:52.530
There's a lot of different
data persistence mechanisms

00:03:52.530 --> 00:03:55.860
available on data cloud,
but BigQuery I believe

00:03:55.860 --> 00:03:58.740
is uniquely well-suited
to the problems warehouses

00:03:58.740 --> 00:04:00.660
are faced with today.

00:04:00.660 --> 00:04:05.400
BigQuery is Google's server
data warehouse in the cloud,

00:04:05.400 --> 00:04:09.980
and it's built to excel
with analytics workloads.

00:04:09.980 --> 00:04:14.200
So let's say you have your
data publishing in the cloud,

00:04:14.200 --> 00:04:17.589
you have it formatted, you
have it synced to BigQuery.

00:04:17.589 --> 00:04:20.950
You're quickly faced with
the next daunting task.

00:04:20.950 --> 00:04:23.380
There's so many things
that you could potentially

00:04:23.380 --> 00:04:26.890
change in your warehouse to try
and optimize your pick rates,

00:04:26.890 --> 00:04:32.230
or find bottlenecks, or just
overall get your throughput up.

00:04:32.230 --> 00:04:35.020
You could potentially
be spending man months

00:04:35.020 --> 00:04:39.280
on a part of your system
that, even if done perfectly,

00:04:39.280 --> 00:04:41.440
would only shave a
small amount of time

00:04:41.440 --> 00:04:43.600
off your average pick
rate, where there

00:04:43.600 --> 00:04:46.930
may be a bottleneck elsewhere
that if you spent a couple man

00:04:46.930 --> 00:04:52.120
hours you would unlock a whole
bunch more at throughput.

00:04:52.120 --> 00:04:54.940
The quickest way to get
to this insight is--

00:04:54.940 --> 00:04:57.160
since your data is
in BigQuery, you

00:04:57.160 --> 00:05:00.910
can hook it up to Data Studio
or many of the other business

00:05:00.910 --> 00:05:04.210
intelligence suites out
there to visualize your data

00:05:04.210 --> 00:05:07.660
and understand where
the time is being spent.

00:05:07.660 --> 00:05:09.970
Armed with these
visualizations, you'd

00:05:09.970 --> 00:05:12.430
know that spending
man months trying

00:05:12.430 --> 00:05:14.260
to make your user
interface on your robot

00:05:14.260 --> 00:05:16.630
more effective for
the picker might not

00:05:16.630 --> 00:05:19.270
be as impactful as
maybe increasing

00:05:19.270 --> 00:05:21.310
the speed of your
robot, or your object

00:05:21.310 --> 00:05:24.460
avoidance, or your
slotting of your products,

00:05:24.460 --> 00:05:26.920
because move time dwarfs
the amount of time

00:05:26.920 --> 00:05:30.590
that it takes to pick a
product out of the box.

00:05:30.590 --> 00:05:32.950
So there's a certain
level of insight

00:05:32.950 --> 00:05:34.990
you could get to
with dashboards,

00:05:34.990 --> 00:05:38.680
but to potentially move
beyond, you can try and employ

00:05:38.680 --> 00:05:40.840
machine learning.

00:05:40.840 --> 00:05:43.570
Machine learning might
uncover different patterns

00:05:43.570 --> 00:05:45.220
in the data that
might not be readily

00:05:45.220 --> 00:05:49.540
apparent with a dashboard
or visualization.

00:05:49.540 --> 00:05:53.140
It also may help in
not only leading you

00:05:53.140 --> 00:05:55.000
to the opportunities
for improvement,

00:05:55.000 --> 00:05:57.370
but be the improvement itself.

00:05:57.370 --> 00:05:59.330
You can potentially
create models

00:05:59.330 --> 00:06:01.840
you can deploy on
a robot, and have

00:06:01.840 --> 00:06:06.610
that robot be more effective
in its day to day job.

00:06:06.610 --> 00:06:09.130
One of the best things
about Google Cloud

00:06:09.130 --> 00:06:11.200
and machine learning
on Google Cloud

00:06:11.200 --> 00:06:14.890
is you don't need an army of
data scientists or TensorFlow

00:06:14.890 --> 00:06:18.520
developers to be able to
start to take advantage of it.

00:06:18.520 --> 00:06:20.470
Since your data is
in BigQuery, you

00:06:20.470 --> 00:06:23.830
can use BigQuery AutoML to
write a simple statement that,

00:06:23.830 --> 00:06:27.700
say, returns your pick times
and all of the different facets

00:06:27.700 --> 00:06:30.150
that might affect
that pick time.

00:06:30.150 --> 00:06:33.310
And then with two
extra lines of code,

00:06:33.310 --> 00:06:35.680
you can generate
a TensorFlow model

00:06:35.680 --> 00:06:37.930
that forecasts
that pick time when

00:06:37.930 --> 00:06:40.780
you change some of the
other parameters around it.

00:06:40.780 --> 00:06:45.880
And just like that, you might
know the effect of the change

00:06:45.880 --> 00:06:48.310
that you intend to
make on your system.

00:06:48.310 --> 00:06:50.860
You can take the
same data in BigQuery

00:06:50.860 --> 00:06:54.040
and return robot
failures and come up

00:06:54.040 --> 00:06:56.710
with a model that gives
you predictive maintenance.

00:06:56.710 --> 00:06:59.770
You can use none of that data
and use a pre-canned model that

00:06:59.770 --> 00:07:01.780
would give a robot
the ability to see

00:07:01.780 --> 00:07:05.910
whether an obstacle in
its way is human or not.

00:07:05.910 --> 00:07:09.110
And for those of you that
aren't in the robotics space,

00:07:09.110 --> 00:07:12.050
this is valuable
because a robot moving

00:07:12.050 --> 00:07:14.960
very fast in close
proximity to a human

00:07:14.960 --> 00:07:18.600
would make that human
very uncomfortable, very

00:07:18.600 --> 00:07:20.110
understandably so.

00:07:20.110 --> 00:07:23.780
But if a robot can't
deduce whether or not

00:07:23.780 --> 00:07:25.770
an obstacle is
human, it would have

00:07:25.770 --> 00:07:29.510
to slow down for every road
cone, every piece of box,

00:07:29.510 --> 00:07:33.180
or anything in its way,
and that can dramatically

00:07:33.180 --> 00:07:37.360
impact the pick rates.

00:07:37.360 --> 00:07:40.420
So let's say you have your
data, it's in the cloud,

00:07:40.420 --> 00:07:42.670
it's in BigQuery, you
have your visualizations,

00:07:42.670 --> 00:07:44.320
and you started to
pick things that you

00:07:44.320 --> 00:07:47.980
want to change about your
system to optimize it.

00:07:47.980 --> 00:07:49.570
Now you're faced
with another problem,

00:07:49.570 --> 00:07:53.110
because to be able to get
the data you need to validate

00:07:53.110 --> 00:07:54.790
those changes are,
in fact, having

00:07:54.790 --> 00:07:58.300
a positive impact on your
system could be costly.

00:07:58.300 --> 00:08:01.480
You can't change the size of
your warehouse very easily,

00:08:01.480 --> 00:08:07.720
or scale up your robot fleet
for one day to 3, 4, or 5x.

00:08:07.720 --> 00:08:11.900
It's pretty cost prohibitive
to do things like that.

00:08:11.900 --> 00:08:17.030
So this is where Google
Kubernetes engine can help.

00:08:17.030 --> 00:08:20.750
You can spool up simulated
robots in simulated warehouses

00:08:20.750 --> 00:08:23.870
and fuel BigQuery with the
same types of data you would

00:08:23.870 --> 00:08:26.390
get if those robots were real.

00:08:26.390 --> 00:08:28.850
That then fuels
the same dashboards

00:08:28.850 --> 00:08:31.070
that are used to pick
what optimizations

00:08:31.070 --> 00:08:33.429
you wanted to take.

00:08:33.429 --> 00:08:36.330
So this is how you can
quickly validate your change

00:08:36.330 --> 00:08:37.620
and iterate onto the next.

00:08:40.130 --> 00:08:41.809
Now, before I hand
over to Chris,

00:08:41.809 --> 00:08:44.480
I want to quickly
introduce 6 River Systems.

00:08:44.480 --> 00:08:47.420
6 River was founded
on the idea that

00:08:47.420 --> 00:08:49.310
the technological
advantages that

00:08:49.310 --> 00:08:52.700
are seen in the large,
million square foot warehouses

00:08:52.700 --> 00:08:56.330
by the commerce giants
should be available to all.

00:08:56.330 --> 00:09:00.380
So not only should these robotic
systems scale up very well,

00:09:00.380 --> 00:09:03.560
they should be
able to scale down.

00:09:03.560 --> 00:09:08.090
Traditionally to get a fleet
of robots in a 500-1,000 square

00:09:08.090 --> 00:09:12.740
foot warehouse, you're talking
about 6 months to 12 months.

00:09:12.740 --> 00:09:17.420
You're talking about many, many
years before the ROI pans out.

00:09:17.420 --> 00:09:20.630
These types of upfront costs
don't work for the 50,000

00:09:20.630 --> 00:09:22.490
square foot warehouse.

00:09:22.490 --> 00:09:26.270
So 6 River has a system that can
be installed and up and running

00:09:26.270 --> 00:09:30.590
in weeks and fluctuate the size
of your fleet for peak times

00:09:30.590 --> 00:09:33.993
and get ROI a lot quicker.

00:09:33.993 --> 00:09:35.660
What they're trying
to do is, in effect,

00:09:35.660 --> 00:09:40.060
democratize robotics and
level the playing field.

00:09:40.060 --> 00:09:42.590
With that, I'll hand
it over to Chris.

00:09:42.590 --> 00:09:44.360
CHRISTOPHER CACIOPPO:
Thank you, Paul.

00:09:44.360 --> 00:09:47.120
So why did we decide to
use collaborative robotics

00:09:47.120 --> 00:09:48.328
to solve a warehouse problem?

00:09:48.328 --> 00:09:49.703
And at the end of
the day, I want

00:09:49.703 --> 00:09:51.890
to give you a quick idea
of the problem space,

00:09:51.890 --> 00:09:54.530
and then we'll talk about that.

00:09:54.530 --> 00:09:57.980
Up until about 10 years
ago, distribution centers

00:09:57.980 --> 00:09:59.870
were places where
manufacturers bought

00:09:59.870 --> 00:10:01.730
a lot of cases of products.

00:10:01.730 --> 00:10:04.910
They would then separate out,
and then ship it off to stores

00:10:04.910 --> 00:10:06.090
to then fill their stocks.

00:10:06.090 --> 00:10:08.090
So there was pretty much
moving cases from place

00:10:08.090 --> 00:10:11.030
to place, which is
reasonably easy to automate.

00:10:11.030 --> 00:10:13.450
They're nice and easy
to touch and square,

00:10:13.450 --> 00:10:15.530
and it didn't require
a ton of labor.

00:10:15.530 --> 00:10:19.400
But in the last 10 years
things have changed.

00:10:19.400 --> 00:10:21.470
When you guys order
things online,

00:10:21.470 --> 00:10:23.670
you never want a
case of toothbrushes.

00:10:23.670 --> 00:10:25.670
You want a toothbrush
and a toothpaste,

00:10:25.670 --> 00:10:27.320
maybe an iPad, some diapers.

00:10:27.320 --> 00:10:28.860
They're all different
for everybody.

00:10:28.860 --> 00:10:31.040
And so it requires
breaking open cases,

00:10:31.040 --> 00:10:33.840
taking things apart, dealing
with odd-shaped items.

00:10:33.840 --> 00:10:38.010
And so, the warehouses
look more like this.

00:10:38.010 --> 00:10:41.030
So there are people working
on automatic hands and arms,

00:10:41.030 --> 00:10:43.000
and you've probably
seen picking challenges,

00:10:43.000 --> 00:10:45.200
and they always pick off
nice clean pick faces.

00:10:45.200 --> 00:10:48.610
But it's clearly for us at least
5 or 10 years off before they

00:10:48.610 --> 00:10:51.140
have robotic arms, they can
go in, take things apart,

00:10:51.140 --> 00:10:53.937
pull things together,
gift wrap, all that stuff

00:10:53.937 --> 00:10:54.770
that requires labor.

00:10:54.770 --> 00:10:57.020
So there's tremendous
labor impact

00:10:57.020 --> 00:11:00.320
for the foreseeable
future in this area.

00:11:00.320 --> 00:11:06.840
To make matters worse, when I
was a kid, it was my birthday.

00:11:06.840 --> 00:11:08.715
My mom orders off
the Sears catalog.

00:11:08.715 --> 00:11:11.090
And we'd mail it in, and it
would take six to eight weeks

00:11:11.090 --> 00:11:13.132
to show up, and I would've
forgotten what it was.

00:11:13.132 --> 00:11:15.230
But I'm sure I was
excited to get it anyway.

00:11:15.230 --> 00:11:17.925
Well in the '90s, early
2000s, shipping times

00:11:17.925 --> 00:11:18.800
were about two weeks.

00:11:18.800 --> 00:11:20.450
You'd order to be online
for early e-commerce,

00:11:20.450 --> 00:11:22.367
they show up in two
weeks, everyone was happy,

00:11:22.367 --> 00:11:23.720
it was great.

00:11:23.720 --> 00:11:26.960
But today's day and age,
people expect things--

00:11:26.960 --> 00:11:28.910
entry level is
two-day shipping, then

00:11:28.910 --> 00:11:31.360
it's like one-day, and like
it'd be nice if you showed up

00:11:31.360 --> 00:11:32.265
that afternoon.

00:11:32.265 --> 00:11:34.640
And so, the pressure that puts
on the fulfillment centers

00:11:34.640 --> 00:11:36.690
to get things out the
door is tremendous.

00:11:36.690 --> 00:11:40.160
So like many things,
as latency goes down,

00:11:40.160 --> 00:11:42.330
the efficiencies get worse.

00:11:42.330 --> 00:11:45.110
And so, there's even
more pressure on labor.

00:11:45.110 --> 00:11:47.390
These fulfillment centers
and distribution centers

00:11:47.390 --> 00:11:50.210
are located in places
where land is inexpensive,

00:11:50.210 --> 00:11:52.070
and they didn't
need a lot of labor,

00:11:52.070 --> 00:11:55.077
but they've kind of sucked
up all the available labor.

00:11:55.077 --> 00:11:57.410
I was with a customer about
two weeks ago and they said,

00:11:57.410 --> 00:11:59.600
it's not so much I care
about the price of labor--

00:11:59.600 --> 00:12:01.640
though I'm sure they do--
it's that I literally

00:12:01.640 --> 00:12:03.560
can't get people to
reliably show up anymore.

00:12:03.560 --> 00:12:05.050
I can't get enough people to do.

00:12:05.050 --> 00:12:09.710
So what we've done is to
solve this problem with Chuck.

00:12:09.710 --> 00:12:12.570
Chuck, do you want
to come over here?

00:12:12.570 --> 00:12:16.260
So Chuck is dressed
up in its Google best.

00:12:16.260 --> 00:12:19.740
Chuck is a collaborative
robotic robot,

00:12:19.740 --> 00:12:21.960
and the thing I want
to point out here

00:12:21.960 --> 00:12:26.280
is this makes the associates'
lives much easier.

00:12:26.280 --> 00:12:30.270
But everyone in our company
is super excited about robots,

00:12:30.270 --> 00:12:31.770
and I think most
people in this talk

00:12:31.770 --> 00:12:33.332
would think robots
are pretty cool.

00:12:33.332 --> 00:12:34.290
Some cameras out there.

00:12:34.290 --> 00:12:35.082
That's pretty cool.

00:12:35.082 --> 00:12:36.870
But people that are
often associates

00:12:36.870 --> 00:12:38.430
working in warehouses,
robots can be

00:12:38.430 --> 00:12:40.410
scary for a number of reasons.

00:12:40.410 --> 00:12:42.000
And so one of the
things we did is--

00:12:42.000 --> 00:12:44.083
when I started this, I
didn't want a little circle

00:12:44.083 --> 00:12:46.670
or rectangle or square in a
warehouse that wasn't obvious

00:12:46.670 --> 00:12:47.670
what it was going to do.

00:12:47.670 --> 00:12:52.230
So I put out on my industrial
engineering hat and said,

00:12:52.230 --> 00:12:54.360
OK, we've got a good
designer, and said,

00:12:54.360 --> 00:12:55.590
I want this to be obvious.

00:12:55.590 --> 00:12:57.780
So this is [INAUDIBLE]
and modeled after a car.

00:12:57.780 --> 00:13:00.120
It's got headlights
and taillights.

00:13:00.120 --> 00:13:01.230
It looks like a car.

00:13:01.230 --> 00:13:02.937
And when you look
at it, no one thinks

00:13:02.937 --> 00:13:04.020
it's going to go that way.

00:13:04.020 --> 00:13:05.280
It's going to go that way.

00:13:05.280 --> 00:13:07.622
And if it backs up, which
it does very rarely,

00:13:07.622 --> 00:13:09.330
it's going to beep
and look like a truck.

00:13:09.330 --> 00:13:10.788
So what we want to
do is get people

00:13:10.788 --> 00:13:12.413
comfortable with the
concept so they're

00:13:12.413 --> 00:13:14.246
comfortable [? with it. ?]
If people are not

00:13:14.246 --> 00:13:16.170
comfortable with their
collaborative robotics,

00:13:16.170 --> 00:13:18.480
they're not going to use it, and
nothing's going to work well.

00:13:18.480 --> 00:13:20.070
So we've had very good
luck, and everyone

00:13:20.070 --> 00:13:21.090
loves using our robots.

00:13:25.390 --> 00:13:29.550
So the way a typical warehouse
works for cart pick operations,

00:13:29.550 --> 00:13:32.220
an associate will walk
over to an [INAUDIBLE]

00:13:32.220 --> 00:13:33.840
with a big heavy
cart, and they'll

00:13:33.840 --> 00:13:35.550
put a whole bunch of
boxes on it and get

00:13:35.550 --> 00:13:37.680
a whole bunch of paper
tickets and walk over

00:13:37.680 --> 00:13:38.940
to their picking racks.

00:13:38.940 --> 00:13:42.930
It'll look like a Costco, or
back of an Ikea, or Sam's Club,

00:13:42.930 --> 00:13:44.110
except much more dense.

00:13:44.110 --> 00:13:45.600
They're then going to walk
up and down a whole bunch

00:13:45.600 --> 00:13:48.225
aisles fulfilling the orders of
these paper tickets and finding

00:13:48.225 --> 00:13:49.530
everything where they remember.

00:13:49.530 --> 00:13:50.947
And then they're
going to bring it

00:13:50.947 --> 00:13:53.640
over to a take off area,
or possibly multiple ones,

00:13:53.640 --> 00:13:55.980
and [? then ?] that big loop.

00:13:55.980 --> 00:13:58.020
At 6 River, we decided
what's most important

00:13:58.020 --> 00:14:00.600
that the associates can do
much better anywhere else

00:14:00.600 --> 00:14:01.860
is the grasping the hands.

00:14:01.860 --> 00:14:05.290
We want to try to alleviate
as much of the other stuff

00:14:05.290 --> 00:14:06.910
they're doing as possible.

00:14:06.910 --> 00:14:08.250
And these aren't small places.

00:14:08.250 --> 00:14:12.510
As we talked about, these are
50k to 500k or more warehouses.

00:14:12.510 --> 00:14:15.850
They're tremendous
amount of movement.

00:14:15.850 --> 00:14:17.730
So what our system
does is there's

00:14:17.730 --> 00:14:20.250
a person who sits in
an induct area on top,

00:14:20.250 --> 00:14:24.060
and they put work on the robot,
and then the robot takes off.

00:14:24.060 --> 00:14:26.820
The robot will then meet
someone in the picking area.

00:14:26.820 --> 00:14:28.830
They will then walk with
them through the area

00:14:28.830 --> 00:14:30.830
showing the pictures,
taking from place to place

00:14:30.830 --> 00:14:32.820
in the best method possible.

00:14:32.820 --> 00:14:34.650
And then when they're
done, that robot

00:14:34.650 --> 00:14:37.310
will take off and deliver it
to multiple places potentially,

00:14:37.310 --> 00:14:38.800
and then another robot
will be waiting for them.

00:14:38.800 --> 00:14:40.920
So we'll keep them
localized in doing what they

00:14:40.920 --> 00:14:44.040
do best in the picking aisles.

00:14:44.040 --> 00:14:46.230
What we can do is
improve pick rates

00:14:46.230 --> 00:14:49.362
2 to 3x over
traditional cart pick,

00:14:49.362 --> 00:14:50.820
and we do this by
a number of ways.

00:14:50.820 --> 00:14:52.298
We reduce the long
walks so people

00:14:52.298 --> 00:14:54.090
don't have to walk
across giant warehouses.

00:14:54.090 --> 00:14:55.710
We very effectively
pick orders that

00:14:55.710 --> 00:14:57.540
go together in a nice
clean way so they

00:14:57.540 --> 00:14:59.650
walk as little as possible.

00:14:59.650 --> 00:15:01.810
We pick them optimally
from place to place.

00:15:01.810 --> 00:15:04.110
And we also have
very clean UIs which

00:15:04.110 --> 00:15:06.570
lets them get the data fast
so they can pick faster,

00:15:06.570 --> 00:15:09.960
they can lower error rates,
and they can move training time

00:15:09.960 --> 00:15:12.570
from weeks to hours.

00:15:12.570 --> 00:15:16.227
And this has been hugely
positive for our customers.

00:15:16.227 --> 00:15:18.310
The next thing about Chuck
that's very interesting

00:15:18.310 --> 00:15:20.030
is it's a disruptive technology.

00:15:20.030 --> 00:15:22.570
So there is a number of
technologies out there

00:15:22.570 --> 00:15:26.630
that allow cart picking
that enhance cart picking

00:15:26.630 --> 00:15:29.020
or replace cart picking.

00:15:29.020 --> 00:15:32.650
But most of these operations
have three to five years ROI,

00:15:32.650 --> 00:15:34.092
and are bolted into the floor.

00:15:34.092 --> 00:15:36.050
And I don't know about
anyone in this audience,

00:15:36.050 --> 00:15:37.710
but I have no idea
where my company's going

00:15:37.710 --> 00:15:38.918
to be in three to five years.

00:15:38.918 --> 00:15:40.640
Like, technology
changes so fast.

00:15:40.640 --> 00:15:42.820
It is a tremendous
investment to do.

00:15:42.820 --> 00:15:47.410
To make matters worse,
generally in this industry,

00:15:47.410 --> 00:15:50.080
there's a huge seasonal change.

00:15:50.080 --> 00:15:52.990
So right around holiday
season, most of our customers

00:15:52.990 --> 00:15:57.040
see a 3 to 7x change in their
operation, tremendous changes.

00:15:57.040 --> 00:16:00.310
So not only do you have
to predict potentially

00:16:00.310 --> 00:16:01.820
five or more years
in the future,

00:16:01.820 --> 00:16:04.450
but you also get the scale up so
that your operation is running

00:16:04.450 --> 00:16:08.200
pretty much at peak, peak
time, and then 80% of your time

00:16:08.200 --> 00:16:11.060
it's significantly
underutilized.

00:16:11.060 --> 00:16:14.260
So what we allow to happen
is we have a two to one

00:16:14.260 --> 00:16:16.720
year ROI for our products.

00:16:16.720 --> 00:16:18.790
They can, instead
of being installed,

00:16:18.790 --> 00:16:22.510
most automation is six months
to a year of installation time.

00:16:22.510 --> 00:16:25.220
We get up and running
in one or two weeks.

00:16:25.220 --> 00:16:27.060
It also allows you to
scale your operation.

00:16:27.060 --> 00:16:29.560
If your operation is getting
more and more, going up and up,

00:16:29.560 --> 00:16:30.610
you just buy more robots.

00:16:30.610 --> 00:16:32.590
We just deliver and
they work as-is.

00:16:32.590 --> 00:16:35.062
If your operation's
scaling down, well,

00:16:35.062 --> 00:16:37.270
if you have another DC or
another fulfillment center,

00:16:37.270 --> 00:16:39.760
just move the robots.

00:16:39.760 --> 00:16:43.480
If you have seasonality, instead
of building our system from 6

00:16:43.480 --> 00:16:46.180
River that works from your
peak for the entire year,

00:16:46.180 --> 00:16:47.680
just build your
average for the year

00:16:47.680 --> 00:16:49.805
and we'll lease you more
robots for your peak time.

00:16:49.805 --> 00:16:52.030
So we have a tremendous
amount of flexibility

00:16:52.030 --> 00:16:55.930
that is just not possible
anywhere else in the industry.

00:16:55.930 --> 00:16:59.590
So from the first day I sat down
at my dining room table with

00:16:59.590 --> 00:17:02.200
my two other co-founders to
the day we had a small fleet

00:17:02.200 --> 00:17:04.240
of collaborative robots
picking live orders--

00:17:04.240 --> 00:17:05.720
I believe it was toys--

00:17:05.720 --> 00:17:09.130
in a customer warehouse,
was nine months, which is,

00:17:09.130 --> 00:17:11.319
looking back on it,
felt much longer,

00:17:11.319 --> 00:17:13.069
but it's an amazing feat.

00:17:13.069 --> 00:17:14.579
And I don't think
this is possible

00:17:14.579 --> 00:17:17.283
10 years ago, even with the best
group of people you could have.

00:17:17.283 --> 00:17:18.700
And the reason is,
I think there's

00:17:18.700 --> 00:17:22.359
three key technologies
of the last 10 years

00:17:22.359 --> 00:17:25.150
that have made a
tremendous change.

00:17:25.150 --> 00:17:27.440
One is ubiquitous
computing power.

00:17:27.440 --> 00:17:31.498
So we've invested in
Nvidia's Jetson TX2 platform,

00:17:31.498 --> 00:17:33.040
and so that gives
a tremendous amount

00:17:33.040 --> 00:17:36.070
of processing power at
very low energy consumption

00:17:36.070 --> 00:17:37.318
at a reasonable price point.

00:17:37.318 --> 00:17:39.110
And so, we've done a
lot of work with them.

00:17:39.110 --> 00:17:40.180
But it's not just that.

00:17:40.180 --> 00:17:42.490
It's all the sensors
and all the things

00:17:42.490 --> 00:17:44.830
that allow robotics to work
have come down tremendously

00:17:44.830 --> 00:17:45.730
in price.

00:17:45.730 --> 00:17:48.850
It wasn't that long ago that
the spinning [? lighters ?]

00:17:48.850 --> 00:17:50.500
you see on top of
the Google cars--

00:17:50.500 --> 00:17:51.978
they cost like 50 grand.

00:17:51.978 --> 00:17:53.770
But now we can have
ones in the robots that

00:17:53.770 --> 00:17:55.030
cost less than 1,000.

00:17:55.030 --> 00:17:57.490
So there's a huge
change in technology

00:17:57.490 --> 00:18:01.450
that's enabling things that
just weren't possible before.

00:18:01.450 --> 00:18:02.950
The second one is
open source, which

00:18:02.950 --> 00:18:04.852
I'm sure everyone here uses.

00:18:04.852 --> 00:18:07.060
It's gotten better and better
over the last 10 years.

00:18:07.060 --> 00:18:08.300
I want to call up ROS.

00:18:08.300 --> 00:18:12.490
ROS is Robot Operating System,
for you non-roboticists.

00:18:12.490 --> 00:18:13.960
It allows you to
bootstrap robots

00:18:13.960 --> 00:18:15.730
really fast so you can
focus on the things

00:18:15.730 --> 00:18:17.980
that your business
cares about, and not

00:18:17.980 --> 00:18:20.180
focusing on the plumbing
and infrastructure.

00:18:20.180 --> 00:18:22.640
So we can very quickly get
a robot up and working,

00:18:22.640 --> 00:18:26.080
and then pile more stuff
on top to optimize it

00:18:26.080 --> 00:18:28.480
for our environments.

00:18:28.480 --> 00:18:32.470
And last but not least is
ubiquitous cloud platforms,

00:18:32.470 --> 00:18:35.770
and we've embraced Google
Cloud from day one.

00:18:35.770 --> 00:18:39.100
This allows you to
scale the operations,

00:18:39.100 --> 00:18:41.398
leverage your
engineers we have, do

00:18:41.398 --> 00:18:43.690
a lot of testing stuff we
have, a lot of secure things.

00:18:43.690 --> 00:18:47.140
It just allows us to go so
much faster than we would have

00:18:47.140 --> 00:18:48.790
otherwise.

00:18:48.790 --> 00:18:50.410
Given the audience,
I think we'll

00:18:50.410 --> 00:18:52.827
spend the rest of the time
talking about the Google Cloud.

00:18:54.315 --> 00:18:56.440
So one thing that's very
important to me personally

00:18:56.440 --> 00:18:58.270
is data-driven decisions.

00:18:58.270 --> 00:19:00.550
Pretty much this
entire conference

00:19:00.550 --> 00:19:02.920
is about data, and
so collecting data,

00:19:02.920 --> 00:19:04.400
and everyone knows data is good.

00:19:04.400 --> 00:19:05.630
But how do you use data?

00:19:05.630 --> 00:19:08.510
How do you make it
effective for you?

00:19:08.510 --> 00:19:10.702
So let me go through some
of the ways we use it.

00:19:10.702 --> 00:19:12.910
And we have a lot of data,
both on the logistics side

00:19:12.910 --> 00:19:14.140
and also from the robot side.

00:19:14.140 --> 00:19:15.790
If you have worked
with robots, robots

00:19:15.790 --> 00:19:17.290
generate a tremendous
amount of data

00:19:17.290 --> 00:19:19.480
and make lots of decisions,
and it's very hard

00:19:19.480 --> 00:19:23.170
to turn churn through
all the data otherwise.

00:19:23.170 --> 00:19:26.960
The first thing we do is
operational intelligence.

00:19:26.960 --> 00:19:30.120
We have a ton of data on all
of our customers' operation,

00:19:30.120 --> 00:19:32.050
and we can tell
them how much work

00:19:32.050 --> 00:19:34.390
they're doing, how much
time they have left,

00:19:34.390 --> 00:19:36.940
the rates they do things,
a whole bunch of stuff.

00:19:36.940 --> 00:19:38.620
We can say which
of their associates

00:19:38.620 --> 00:19:40.120
are performing better and worse.

00:19:40.120 --> 00:19:43.270
But even more than
that, instead of saying,

00:19:43.270 --> 00:19:46.090
Chris is underperforming,
I can say,

00:19:46.090 --> 00:19:47.590
Chris is doing pretty well.

00:19:47.590 --> 00:19:49.193
He's just really
slow at scanning.

00:19:49.193 --> 00:19:50.860
Maybe he doesn't how
that section works.

00:19:50.860 --> 00:19:52.060
And so, an associate
can come to me

00:19:52.060 --> 00:19:53.518
and help coach me
through something

00:19:53.518 --> 00:19:56.135
that I'm weak at as opposed
to just dealing with it,

00:19:56.135 --> 00:19:58.510
or deciding they should get
some other employee if that's

00:19:58.510 --> 00:19:59.140
even possible.

00:20:02.140 --> 00:20:04.530
The next thing is
visualizing data.

00:20:04.530 --> 00:20:05.970
This is one of my
favorite things.

00:20:05.970 --> 00:20:10.100
So this up here
is a picture of--

00:20:10.100 --> 00:20:12.210
our robots go through the
warehouse all the time.

00:20:12.210 --> 00:20:15.570
They report back wireless
signal strength constantly,

00:20:15.570 --> 00:20:16.802
so every second or so.

00:20:16.802 --> 00:20:19.010
And so, this is a map of
the wireless signal strength

00:20:19.010 --> 00:20:20.460
at various parts of warehouse.

00:20:20.460 --> 00:20:22.260
So people do wireless
surveys, and they're

00:20:22.260 --> 00:20:24.620
sort of ad hoc and
occasionally [? happen. ?]

00:20:24.620 --> 00:20:26.048
But we are
constantly, real time,

00:20:26.048 --> 00:20:27.840
showing a wireless
survey of the warehouse.

00:20:27.840 --> 00:20:29.298
So you can tell
in this warehouse,

00:20:29.298 --> 00:20:31.840
there's a big area in the center
that's probably pretty weak,

00:20:31.840 --> 00:20:34.298
and if the customer cares they
should probably update that.

00:20:36.400 --> 00:20:38.640
But I think what's
more important is

00:20:38.640 --> 00:20:41.940
how we use it for our internal
engineering personally.

00:20:41.940 --> 00:20:44.440
So when you're starting to build
the robot, you get a robot,

00:20:44.440 --> 00:20:46.273
and you get-- what we
have to do first-- you

00:20:46.273 --> 00:20:47.250
have to make it move.

00:20:47.250 --> 00:20:48.925
Check, it moves.

00:20:48.925 --> 00:20:50.550
And then we don't
just want it to move,

00:20:50.550 --> 00:20:52.110
we want it to move
from A to B. So we

00:20:52.110 --> 00:20:53.250
want it to move over there.

00:20:53.250 --> 00:20:54.510
Excellent, check.

00:20:54.510 --> 00:20:56.040
We got it to move
A to B. And then

00:20:56.040 --> 00:20:57.490
you want it not to hit things.

00:20:57.490 --> 00:20:58.370
All right, check.

00:20:58.370 --> 00:20:59.950
And you go through this
whole list of things,

00:20:59.950 --> 00:21:01.530
whether it's robots
or any products

00:21:01.530 --> 00:21:03.960
you guys do until you
get an MVP and it's

00:21:03.960 --> 00:21:06.300
in front of the customer.

00:21:06.300 --> 00:21:07.808
At that point, that's great.

00:21:07.808 --> 00:21:10.350
We get some feedback and we work
on the most important things

00:21:10.350 --> 00:21:12.720
next.

00:21:12.720 --> 00:21:15.140
How do we choose the
most important things?

00:21:15.140 --> 00:21:18.120
In most operations, it's
done in one of three ways.

00:21:18.120 --> 00:21:21.290
One, the manager or
whoever's in charge decides.

00:21:21.290 --> 00:21:23.240
Two, the loudest
engineer decides.

00:21:23.240 --> 00:21:25.780
Or three, if you're doing
things probably correctly,

00:21:25.780 --> 00:21:28.920
you have a consensus of
engineers that decide together.

00:21:28.920 --> 00:21:31.328
But the problem is even
in the latter case,

00:21:31.328 --> 00:21:32.870
where we have a
consensus, it doesn't

00:21:32.870 --> 00:21:34.760
mean you're working on the right
thing or most important thing

00:21:34.760 --> 00:21:35.260
first.

00:21:35.260 --> 00:21:36.260
You just all agree.

00:21:36.260 --> 00:21:38.490
You can all agree
on the wrong thing.

00:21:38.490 --> 00:21:41.870
So this is a graph up
here about the time

00:21:41.870 --> 00:21:44.120
that the associates are
spending on our product, which

00:21:44.120 --> 00:21:47.790
is important of labor
management and labor savings.

00:21:47.790 --> 00:21:49.970
So the big blue box
here is movement.

00:21:49.970 --> 00:21:52.610
It says the largest
amount of time, about 44%,

00:21:52.610 --> 00:21:54.350
is spent on the robot
moving from point A

00:21:54.350 --> 00:21:58.010
to point B with the
associate in tow.

00:21:58.010 --> 00:22:01.070
There are other things
down here to just scanning.

00:22:01.070 --> 00:22:03.770
And so, one of our
engineers made a great idea.

00:22:03.770 --> 00:22:05.543
We can just remove
scanning entirely,

00:22:05.543 --> 00:22:06.960
and that sounds
like a great idea.

00:22:06.960 --> 00:22:07.850
We could just skip that step.

00:22:07.850 --> 00:22:08.490
That would be awesome.

00:22:08.490 --> 00:22:09.260
Let's work on that.

00:22:09.260 --> 00:22:10.718
And someone else
had an idea, says,

00:22:10.718 --> 00:22:12.800
we can reduce movement 20%.

00:22:12.800 --> 00:22:16.220
That doesn't sound as good,
but if you look at the numbers,

00:22:16.220 --> 00:22:18.470
it's way better to spend
time reducing movement by 20%

00:22:18.470 --> 00:22:20.822
than it is removing a stage,
even though it doesn't

00:22:20.822 --> 00:22:22.280
intuitively feel
like just removing

00:22:22.280 --> 00:22:24.900
something isn't a better idea.

00:22:24.900 --> 00:22:28.640
So I'm going to give
an example of that.

00:22:28.640 --> 00:22:30.190
This is another
customer warehouse,

00:22:30.190 --> 00:22:33.050
and instead of plotting
Wi-Fi signal strength,

00:22:33.050 --> 00:22:36.230
we're plotting average
moving speed of the robots.

00:22:36.230 --> 00:22:39.938
So you can see red is
slower, green is faster.

00:22:39.938 --> 00:22:42.230
So if we look at some of this
and analyze a little bit,

00:22:42.230 --> 00:22:44.105
at the end of aisles
things are slowing down.

00:22:44.105 --> 00:22:44.930
It makes sense.

00:22:44.930 --> 00:22:47.300
There's cross traffic,
there's blind turns.

00:22:47.300 --> 00:22:49.175
You got to be a lot more
careful around that.

00:22:49.175 --> 00:22:51.175
But the slower areas in
the middle of the aisles

00:22:51.175 --> 00:22:52.400
doesn't make a lot of sense.

00:22:52.400 --> 00:22:53.942
Robots should be
humming along there.

00:22:53.942 --> 00:22:55.782
It should be fine.

00:22:55.782 --> 00:22:57.990
So we can do two things
here, and we do both of them.

00:22:57.990 --> 00:22:59.990
One is we can push back on the
customers and say, hey, you

00:22:59.990 --> 00:23:01.073
guys should clean this up.

00:23:01.073 --> 00:23:03.410
The robots move faster
and your rates go up.

00:23:03.410 --> 00:23:06.560
But really, it's better if
like-- why are we slowing down?

00:23:06.560 --> 00:23:09.720
Like, because everybody wants
the robots to be faster.

00:23:09.720 --> 00:23:12.050
So I think obviously the
next thing we do is just

00:23:12.050 --> 00:23:14.770
make the robots'
top speed faster.

00:23:14.770 --> 00:23:17.070
Or is it?

00:23:17.070 --> 00:23:19.080
So some of our engineers
took some robots

00:23:19.080 --> 00:23:21.030
and put them on a test track.

00:23:21.030 --> 00:23:24.330
And instead of just reporting
the maximum speed of robots,

00:23:24.330 --> 00:23:27.600
they report the internal
choices of robots.

00:23:27.600 --> 00:23:30.390
And essentially a crash
course in robotics here--

00:23:30.390 --> 00:23:32.460
we have a bunch
of different tasks

00:23:32.460 --> 00:23:34.770
that determine how
fast a robot can go,

00:23:34.770 --> 00:23:36.720
and the slowest one
wins, because we believe

00:23:36.720 --> 00:23:38.590
in safety and everything else.

00:23:38.590 --> 00:23:42.690
So whatever chooses the slowest,
that's the one that we go at.

00:23:42.690 --> 00:23:45.630
So if you look up here, red
says I'm near an obstacle.

00:23:45.630 --> 00:23:47.790
Yellow says go top speed.

00:23:47.790 --> 00:23:50.490
So if you look up there and
we increase our top speed,

00:23:50.490 --> 00:23:53.377
really wouldn't have had a giant
impact on a robot right now.

00:23:53.377 --> 00:23:54.960
Because mostly we
[INAUDIBLE] objects,

00:23:54.960 --> 00:23:56.877
and we're slowing ourselves
down because we're

00:23:56.877 --> 00:23:58.320
being extra careful.

00:23:58.320 --> 00:24:01.370
So the engineers took this
data and attacking that problem

00:24:01.370 --> 00:24:03.453
instead of trying to make
the robot respond faster

00:24:03.453 --> 00:24:06.760
to go faster, and
analyze that problem.

00:24:06.760 --> 00:24:09.957
And after a couple of weeks
they did the test track again,

00:24:09.957 --> 00:24:11.040
and this is what they got.

00:24:11.040 --> 00:24:14.770
You'll notice instead of being
like 85% red, it's now like 40%

00:24:14.770 --> 00:24:15.270
red.

00:24:15.270 --> 00:24:17.490
And it's a lot of yellow,
which is the top speed.

00:24:17.490 --> 00:24:19.650
The robot's just cruising
along at top speed.

00:24:19.650 --> 00:24:22.430
So there was a huge impact
of diving into this problem,

00:24:22.430 --> 00:24:26.440
and it resulted in about a
15% movement speed increase.

00:24:26.440 --> 00:24:28.860
And now that the engineers
can look at this and say, OK,

00:24:28.860 --> 00:24:30.900
should we keep pushing
on that problem?

00:24:30.900 --> 00:24:33.870
Should we look at the teal over
here, which is a pathing issue?

00:24:33.870 --> 00:24:35.760
Should we think about
raising top speed?

00:24:35.760 --> 00:24:37.530
But it allows a
data-driven decision,

00:24:37.530 --> 00:24:40.350
when obviously everyone says
we should just make the robots

00:24:40.350 --> 00:24:43.480
move faster, and that was
actually the wrong decision.

00:24:43.480 --> 00:24:45.600
So that's my part of the talk.

00:24:45.600 --> 00:24:48.100
I'm going to hand off to Joe,
who makes all the magic happen

00:24:48.100 --> 00:24:48.756
here.

00:24:48.756 --> 00:24:52.230
JOSEPH HUGHES: Thanks, Chris.

00:24:52.230 --> 00:24:57.350
So you just got hired at the
next IoT robotics company,

00:24:57.350 --> 00:24:59.720
and you wake up the next
day and you're feeling good,

00:24:59.720 --> 00:25:03.920
but then you think to yourself,
I need a robot infrastructure.

00:25:03.920 --> 00:25:07.760
Lucky for you, you're
here at Google Cloud Next,

00:25:07.760 --> 00:25:11.030
and we're going to
help you understand

00:25:11.030 --> 00:25:12.800
how we've used Google
Cloud to address

00:25:12.800 --> 00:25:17.030
some of the most challenging
aspects of this problem.

00:25:17.030 --> 00:25:19.130
The first and most
obvious problem

00:25:19.130 --> 00:25:20.930
is you have all this data.

00:25:20.930 --> 00:25:23.330
You have hundreds of
robots in the field.

00:25:23.330 --> 00:25:26.090
You have robots that are
in your test environments

00:25:26.090 --> 00:25:27.777
and your customer environments.

00:25:27.777 --> 00:25:29.360
You have to get all
this data, and you

00:25:29.360 --> 00:25:31.110
have to make some sort
of sense out of it.

00:25:33.200 --> 00:25:35.710
So we're going to talk about
this slide for the next 30

00:25:35.710 --> 00:25:37.218
minutes.

00:25:37.218 --> 00:25:38.260
You guys didn't run away.

00:25:38.260 --> 00:25:39.385
We're not going to do that.

00:25:39.385 --> 00:25:41.350
But I think here there's
an important thing

00:25:41.350 --> 00:25:44.440
that I want to point out
about this architecture.

00:25:44.440 --> 00:25:46.990
We don't just use Cloud
IoT Core for everything.

00:25:46.990 --> 00:25:49.787
We also use Google Cloud
Storage for a couple of things.

00:25:49.787 --> 00:25:52.120
And the reason we do that is
that there's some data that

00:25:52.120 --> 00:25:54.730
is really important to have
in real time, but there's

00:25:54.730 --> 00:25:58.470
other data that we may need
later that is really dense.

00:25:58.470 --> 00:26:01.130
And Chris talked a little
bit about that data.

00:26:01.130 --> 00:26:05.103
That's your LIDAR scans, your
point clouds, the roboticists--

00:26:05.103 --> 00:26:06.520
if you're not a
roboticist, that's

00:26:06.520 --> 00:26:09.160
really kind of what
the robot is seeing,

00:26:09.160 --> 00:26:10.797
and logging all of that.

00:26:10.797 --> 00:26:12.880
And we need to put that
into Google Cloud Storage,

00:26:12.880 --> 00:26:14.890
because those files
are big, they're dense,

00:26:14.890 --> 00:26:17.500
and then we need to run
it and break it down.

00:26:17.500 --> 00:26:21.640
So we make heavy use of
Pub/Sub and Cloud Functions

00:26:21.640 --> 00:26:23.890
to massage that data,
get it into the format

00:26:23.890 --> 00:26:25.180
that we can consume.

00:26:25.180 --> 00:26:30.010
Some of that data comes all the
way back through to a web page

00:26:30.010 --> 00:26:31.660
where people can consume it.

00:26:31.660 --> 00:26:34.048
Some of that data
goes to BigQuery,

00:26:34.048 --> 00:26:36.340
and that's what drives some
of those interesting graphs

00:26:36.340 --> 00:26:38.505
that Chris showed you before.

00:26:38.505 --> 00:26:39.880
What we're going
to be doing next

00:26:39.880 --> 00:26:43.118
is using that data in BigQuery,
as Paul alluded to earlier,

00:26:43.118 --> 00:26:44.660
to drive some machine
learning models

00:26:44.660 --> 00:26:47.140
so we can get better and
think more about the data

00:26:47.140 --> 00:26:49.060
that we're collecting.

00:26:49.060 --> 00:26:52.570
But this infrastructure
for us, this design

00:26:52.570 --> 00:26:56.620
has survived the test of
time and scaled with us

00:26:56.620 --> 00:26:59.240
with no engineering
intervention at all.

00:27:01.908 --> 00:27:03.950
So one of the things that
you want from this data

00:27:03.950 --> 00:27:05.450
is real time location.

00:27:05.450 --> 00:27:07.130
You may think to
yourself, well, why?

00:27:07.130 --> 00:27:08.060
That seems important.

00:27:08.060 --> 00:27:11.240
But I'm going to explain to
you why it's very important.

00:27:11.240 --> 00:27:15.800
If you're managing a million
square foot warehouse and you

00:27:15.800 --> 00:27:18.240
can't have line of
sight to everything.

00:27:18.240 --> 00:27:20.580
So you need a bird's eye
view of what's going on.

00:27:20.580 --> 00:27:22.140
You have 25 trucks in the field.

00:27:22.140 --> 00:27:24.410
You might have 50
employees in the field,

00:27:24.410 --> 00:27:27.810
and you really need
that real time feedback.

00:27:27.810 --> 00:27:30.720
So most obviously, you just
need to know where Chucks are.

00:27:30.720 --> 00:27:33.075
If you can only see
10 Chucks at a time,

00:27:33.075 --> 00:27:34.700
you need to know
where the other 10 are

00:27:34.700 --> 00:27:36.283
and make sure they're
doing the things

00:27:36.283 --> 00:27:40.160
that you expect that they should
be doing in your warehouse.

00:27:40.160 --> 00:27:42.140
You also want to see
if Chuck's in trouble.

00:27:42.140 --> 00:27:44.300
You'll see two Chucks
here blinking red.

00:27:44.300 --> 00:27:47.220
If a warehouse manager was
to click on one of those,

00:27:47.220 --> 00:27:50.630
they could see that there's
an obstacle in Chuck's way,

00:27:50.630 --> 00:27:53.750
or that there's some other
unexpected event, maybe

00:27:53.750 --> 00:27:56.960
some sort of maintenance that
needs to be done to Chuck.

00:27:56.960 --> 00:27:59.988
In any case, it really
calls out very clearly

00:27:59.988 --> 00:28:02.030
that there's a problem
that needs to be resolved.

00:28:02.030 --> 00:28:04.490
They can get on a
radio, talk to someone,

00:28:04.490 --> 00:28:06.380
ask them what the
problem is, or they can

00:28:06.380 --> 00:28:08.100
make the decision to walk over.

00:28:08.100 --> 00:28:11.990
But if you're constantly walking
around a million square foot

00:28:11.990 --> 00:28:14.540
warehouse looking for
problems, most likely

00:28:14.540 --> 00:28:18.100
you're not going to
be able to find them.

00:28:18.100 --> 00:28:20.620
Lastly, you want to
understand the operation.

00:28:20.620 --> 00:28:23.070
So if you see Chucks
queuing up in a weird way,

00:28:23.070 --> 00:28:26.367
or you see Chucks
and your operators

00:28:26.367 --> 00:28:28.950
are not in the right part of the
warehouse for where they need

00:28:28.950 --> 00:28:31.020
to be at that point
of the day, then you

00:28:31.020 --> 00:28:33.910
can correct that and make
sure that your rates--

00:28:33.910 --> 00:28:35.160
you still hit your rates.

00:28:35.160 --> 00:28:37.080
And that means that
everyone in the audience

00:28:37.080 --> 00:28:40.380
gets their shoes on time, or
their toothbrushes on time,

00:28:40.380 --> 00:28:42.620
or whatever you might be
ordering off the internet.

00:28:45.325 --> 00:28:47.450
The next big thing that
you want from all this data

00:28:47.450 --> 00:28:49.010
is real time monitoring.

00:28:49.010 --> 00:28:51.260
Our customers run 24/7.

00:28:51.260 --> 00:28:52.600
The internet's up 24/7.

00:28:52.600 --> 00:28:54.470
People are ordering 24/7.

00:28:54.470 --> 00:28:56.850
And you expect your
stuff in two days.

00:28:56.850 --> 00:29:01.730
So we have a tech support
team that also runs 24/7.

00:29:01.730 --> 00:29:03.410
And when something
comes in, they

00:29:03.410 --> 00:29:05.420
need to really quickly respond.

00:29:05.420 --> 00:29:08.240
Now, for our cloud software
that's running in the cloud,

00:29:08.240 --> 00:29:10.220
Stackdriver was a clear choice.

00:29:10.220 --> 00:29:12.060
Out of the box it
gave us alerting,

00:29:12.060 --> 00:29:14.960
it gave us all the monitoring
that we could possibly want,

00:29:14.960 --> 00:29:16.820
and we didn't have to
invest any engineering

00:29:16.820 --> 00:29:18.260
time into instrumenting.

00:29:18.260 --> 00:29:21.380
It came with the platform.

00:29:21.380 --> 00:29:23.660
We use Google Core
IoT to feed data

00:29:23.660 --> 00:29:26.150
and to Influx DB for a
time series database,

00:29:26.150 --> 00:29:28.130
and then also drive
the dashboards

00:29:28.130 --> 00:29:29.700
like you see behind me.

00:29:29.700 --> 00:29:33.050
This gives us our CPU
metrics, our memory metrics,

00:29:33.050 --> 00:29:34.610
and our temperature metrics.

00:29:34.610 --> 00:29:36.810
Some of these warehouses
get up to over 100 degrees.

00:29:36.810 --> 00:29:40.790
So the temperature of their
CPU becomes super important.

00:29:40.790 --> 00:29:43.760
Your fan fails and
your CPU's overheating,

00:29:43.760 --> 00:29:45.535
you're going to have
a very unhappy robot,

00:29:45.535 --> 00:29:47.660
and eventually you'll have
a very unhappy customer.

00:29:50.180 --> 00:29:52.718
But this really all drives
preventative maintenance.

00:29:52.718 --> 00:29:54.260
If we can take a
Chuck out of service

00:29:54.260 --> 00:29:57.390
before it fails and
clogs up the works,

00:29:57.390 --> 00:29:59.690
then we can save
our customer time.

00:29:59.690 --> 00:30:02.040
They may be running a degraded
system for a little bit.

00:30:02.040 --> 00:30:04.600
But hopefully we can pull
the Chuck out of service,

00:30:04.600 --> 00:30:06.830
replace a part from
our spare part kit,

00:30:06.830 --> 00:30:09.290
and get Chuck back
in and doing its job.

00:30:12.270 --> 00:30:14.330
Lastly, we touched on
this a little earlier,

00:30:14.330 --> 00:30:18.062
but enabling technical
support is super important.

00:30:18.062 --> 00:30:19.770
They're really the
front line of defense,

00:30:19.770 --> 00:30:22.410
and they need tools to
help the customer resolve

00:30:22.410 --> 00:30:25.590
their problems quickly and
not interrupt engineering.

00:30:25.590 --> 00:30:27.480
If engineers are
spending all their time

00:30:27.480 --> 00:30:29.840
solving customer
issues in the field,

00:30:29.840 --> 00:30:31.590
then they're not going
to be able to build

00:30:31.590 --> 00:30:33.990
the next great feature
and make Chuck better.

00:30:33.990 --> 00:30:36.300
And we're constantly
doing the experiments,

00:30:36.300 --> 00:30:38.670
like Chris showed you
earlier, to make Chuck faster

00:30:38.670 --> 00:30:40.440
and to continually
raise our customer's

00:30:40.440 --> 00:30:44.272
rates through just software and
better engineering practices.

00:30:46.950 --> 00:30:49.040
So this is the
second type of data

00:30:49.040 --> 00:30:51.650
that we were talking about
in that architecture slide.

00:30:51.650 --> 00:30:53.930
This is data that's not
really valuable in real time

00:30:53.930 --> 00:30:57.120
from every single Chuck
because it's very dense,

00:30:57.120 --> 00:30:59.960
and it gives you the
whole picture of what

00:30:59.960 --> 00:31:01.910
Chuck sees in the world.

00:31:01.910 --> 00:31:05.810
But you may want
this data because you

00:31:05.810 --> 00:31:07.700
want to replay Chuck travel.

00:31:07.700 --> 00:31:09.960
You get something from
the field and say,

00:31:09.960 --> 00:31:13.820
hey, Chuck is moving
strangely around this shelf,

00:31:13.820 --> 00:31:17.930
or Chuck can't see this
particular thing on the ground,

00:31:17.930 --> 00:31:19.140
or something of that nature.

00:31:19.140 --> 00:31:21.500
And that comes to
our engineering team,

00:31:21.500 --> 00:31:24.600
and we need to be
able to see that.

00:31:24.600 --> 00:31:26.110
And so, we have two options.

00:31:26.110 --> 00:31:27.130
We can get on a plane.

00:31:27.130 --> 00:31:29.270
We can fly over
to the warehouse,

00:31:29.270 --> 00:31:33.050
go take a look at Chuck and say,
oh, well, I guess I can hook in

00:31:33.050 --> 00:31:34.880
and take a look at the sensors.

00:31:34.880 --> 00:31:37.240
Or, maybe it's not even doing
that anymore by the time

00:31:37.240 --> 00:31:39.650
you get there, but
planes are expensive

00:31:39.650 --> 00:31:42.050
and engineering time
is also expensive.

00:31:42.050 --> 00:31:45.860
Or, you can replay this Chuck
travel from Google Cloud,

00:31:45.860 --> 00:31:48.760
you pull that down, and you
can replay in real time.

00:31:48.760 --> 00:31:50.510
So what you're seeing
on the right of this

00:31:50.510 --> 00:31:53.090
is you can actually see
Chuck seeing other Chucks.

00:31:53.090 --> 00:31:54.572
On the left you can see shelves.

00:31:54.572 --> 00:31:56.030
That's what's called
a point cloud.

00:31:56.030 --> 00:31:58.130
You can see it's made
up of a lot of points.

00:31:58.130 --> 00:32:00.080
From this our
engineers can deduce

00:32:00.080 --> 00:32:01.850
a lot of interesting things.

00:32:01.850 --> 00:32:04.040
There's your test
environment which

00:32:04.040 --> 00:32:06.380
you have in your warehouse,
where you do all your tests

00:32:06.380 --> 00:32:09.200
and you try really hard to make
Chuck move the right way all

00:32:09.200 --> 00:32:10.100
the time.

00:32:10.100 --> 00:32:12.440
Inevitably, though, you
get out into the field

00:32:12.440 --> 00:32:15.670
and there's things
you didn't expect.

00:32:15.670 --> 00:32:17.810
We call them movement
issues, and they're caused

00:32:17.810 --> 00:32:19.500
by some interesting things.

00:32:19.500 --> 00:32:23.330
Some customers shine their
floors or they have skylights,

00:32:23.330 --> 00:32:25.220
and that feeds back
into the sensors

00:32:25.220 --> 00:32:27.410
and makes this point
cloud much different

00:32:27.410 --> 00:32:33.420
than it does looking at our
headquarters on our test track.

00:32:33.420 --> 00:32:35.070
Help improve efficiency--

00:32:35.070 --> 00:32:37.060
Chris kind of alluded
to this earlier.

00:32:37.060 --> 00:32:40.200
But if there's a specific
place in the warehouse where

00:32:40.200 --> 00:32:42.150
the Chucks just
always move slow,

00:32:42.150 --> 00:32:45.990
sometimes what we can do is
clearly just see that the LIDAR

00:32:45.990 --> 00:32:48.810
or the camera is seeing
something that it shouldn't.

00:32:48.810 --> 00:32:50.460
And with a little
bit of adjustment

00:32:50.460 --> 00:32:52.890
by the customer, or a
little bit of adjustment

00:32:52.890 --> 00:32:54.930
in the map that we
use to navigate,

00:32:54.930 --> 00:32:56.400
we can alleviate those problems.

00:32:59.050 --> 00:33:02.100
So you have all your
data, but now you

00:33:02.100 --> 00:33:05.610
have a hundred, a thousand, a
million robots in the field,

00:33:05.610 --> 00:33:08.290
and you need to upgrade
them at some point.

00:33:08.290 --> 00:33:11.460
So how do you do that?

00:33:11.460 --> 00:33:16.440
Traditional software management,
Ansible, Saltstack, Puppet,

00:33:16.440 --> 00:33:18.300
they're great tools
for managing servers.

00:33:18.300 --> 00:33:21.630
You have an always great
internet connection,

00:33:21.630 --> 00:33:22.985
and you're always--

00:33:22.985 --> 00:33:25.110
and you can kind of rely
on the internet connection

00:33:25.110 --> 00:33:28.680
to make things good for you.

00:33:28.680 --> 00:33:32.010
The great white whale of
Chuck is reliable Wi-Fi.

00:33:32.010 --> 00:33:34.560
Warehouses just don't
have reliable Wi-Fi.

00:33:34.560 --> 00:33:36.810
There's a lot of racks,
there's a lot of interference.

00:33:36.810 --> 00:33:39.900
And so, what we end up with
is half applied states.

00:33:39.900 --> 00:33:42.450
So almost worse than
an upgrade just failing

00:33:42.450 --> 00:33:45.150
is a half upgrade, an upgrade
that looks like it went good

00:33:45.150 --> 00:33:46.640
but didn't go all the way.

00:33:46.640 --> 00:33:49.920
And we battled a lot of
these problems early on.

00:33:49.920 --> 00:33:53.190
So we said to ourself, there
has to be a better way.

00:33:53.190 --> 00:33:55.710
We have to be able to reduce
the complexity of these things.

00:33:55.710 --> 00:33:59.670
We have to be able to
minimize the amount of data

00:33:59.670 --> 00:34:04.310
that's being pulled down,
and do it more deterministic.

00:34:04.310 --> 00:34:07.510
So we shoehorned
everything into Docker.

00:34:07.510 --> 00:34:10.659
Docker containers are pretty
great at solving this problem.

00:34:10.659 --> 00:34:13.449
If you have a container, it
either downloads or it doesn't.

00:34:13.449 --> 00:34:16.570
If you need to roll back, roll
back to a different container.

00:34:16.570 --> 00:34:19.989
You have a very good view
of the world of where

00:34:19.989 --> 00:34:21.790
your software is updating.

00:34:21.790 --> 00:34:24.790
And so, if you need to
update 1,000 of these things,

00:34:24.790 --> 00:34:27.580
all you need is a good
internet connection

00:34:27.580 --> 00:34:30.969
to download a single file
versus what you would have

00:34:30.969 --> 00:34:34.659
to do in the other situation,
which is a bit of a back

00:34:34.659 --> 00:34:37.909
and forth between a lot
of different services.

00:34:37.909 --> 00:34:39.760
We really leverage
Google Cloud's Platform

00:34:39.760 --> 00:34:41.290
to make this possible.

00:34:41.290 --> 00:34:43.570
And don't get scared, it's
no architecture slide,

00:34:43.570 --> 00:34:45.989
but we're going to
go forth with it.

00:34:45.989 --> 00:34:48.489
Google has got a lot of great
tools for building containers.

00:34:48.489 --> 00:34:50.622
Cloud Build scales
with our developers.

00:34:50.622 --> 00:34:52.330
As they need to make
containers and we're

00:34:52.330 --> 00:34:55.120
rushing to finish
releases, then we

00:34:55.120 --> 00:34:57.820
can have 20 developers doing
20 different builds and Google

00:34:57.820 --> 00:34:58.370
Cloud Build.

00:34:58.370 --> 00:35:02.650
It scales up, we push all those
to the container registry,

00:35:02.650 --> 00:35:06.250
and then we have our custom
solution to this problem.

00:35:06.250 --> 00:35:10.990
But really, Google has a great
toolkit with Kubernetes Engine

00:35:10.990 --> 00:35:14.470
that you can run
software that just

00:35:14.470 --> 00:35:16.870
consumes a lot of this stuff.

00:35:16.870 --> 00:35:19.630
And finally, this software
allows us to push things out

00:35:19.630 --> 00:35:20.900
through IoT Core.

00:35:20.900 --> 00:35:22.390
IoT Core can't
just collect data,

00:35:22.390 --> 00:35:25.460
but you can actually manage the
configuration of the robots.

00:35:25.460 --> 00:35:27.580
So essentially the
message goes out to 1,

00:35:27.580 --> 00:35:30.477
1,000, a million
robots saying, we

00:35:30.477 --> 00:35:32.560
need you to download this
container from Container

00:35:32.560 --> 00:35:35.690
Registry, and then
they go into action.

00:35:35.690 --> 00:35:38.080
So I'm going to show
you how that works.

00:35:38.080 --> 00:35:40.900
Here is an actual upgrade.

00:35:40.900 --> 00:35:45.460
So this is sped up a little bit,
but these containers download.

00:35:45.460 --> 00:35:49.750
The robot gets the command,
gives us the signal

00:35:49.750 --> 00:35:53.580
that it downloaded, and then
we can apply that upgrade.

00:35:53.580 --> 00:35:55.960
And so, you'll see me
do three at a time here.

00:35:55.960 --> 00:35:57.698
They download and they apply.

00:35:57.698 --> 00:35:59.740
And so, we're taking three
Chucks out of service,

00:35:59.740 --> 00:36:02.380
we're downloading a container,
that container gets applied,

00:36:02.380 --> 00:36:03.940
they go back into service.

00:36:03.940 --> 00:36:08.210
But we know exactly if something
didn't work, if it did work,

00:36:08.210 --> 00:36:10.525
and the nice thing
is going from build

00:36:10.525 --> 00:36:14.200
X to build Y is just as easy
as going from build Y back

00:36:14.200 --> 00:36:18.310
to build X. I think all of us
know that sometimes software

00:36:18.310 --> 00:36:19.720
upgrades don't go well.

00:36:19.720 --> 00:36:22.060
So almost as important
as your upgrade path

00:36:22.060 --> 00:36:23.470
is your downgrade path.

00:36:23.470 --> 00:36:29.200
But with Docker, Kubernetes to
drive this software, and IoT

00:36:29.200 --> 00:36:32.620
Core, we can really manage
this problem very well.

00:36:35.410 --> 00:36:39.670
So we have all the data.

00:36:39.670 --> 00:36:42.820
We have thousands of robots.

00:36:42.820 --> 00:36:46.210
And now our customer
comes and says to us,

00:36:46.210 --> 00:36:50.020
hey, we think we need to
rearrange our entire warehouse.

00:36:50.020 --> 00:36:52.840
We have 100 Chucks, and
we think that this design

00:36:52.840 --> 00:36:54.280
might work better.

00:36:54.280 --> 00:36:56.620
And you're like, OK,
well, so we're back

00:36:56.620 --> 00:36:58.690
into kind of a problem here.

00:36:58.690 --> 00:37:00.700
So we can either fly
out to the warehouse,

00:37:00.700 --> 00:37:04.120
bring another 100 Chucks,
get another warehouse,

00:37:04.120 --> 00:37:05.800
set it up the way
they want, test,

00:37:05.800 --> 00:37:09.170
and make sure that it works, or
we have to do something else.

00:37:09.170 --> 00:37:11.590
And I think we all kind
of realize we pretty much

00:37:11.590 --> 00:37:14.930
just have to do something else.

00:37:14.930 --> 00:37:19.090
So going back to our
Docker adventure here,

00:37:19.090 --> 00:37:23.280
we were really pleased when we
got to this point when we said,

00:37:23.280 --> 00:37:25.220
OK, we already have
everything in Docker.

00:37:25.220 --> 00:37:27.890
So all we really
need to do is change

00:37:27.890 --> 00:37:30.530
a few things in this
container, and we'll actually

00:37:30.530 --> 00:37:35.040
have a Docker container that
can be run in a simulated world.

00:37:35.040 --> 00:37:37.610
So we have the robot operating
system running completely

00:37:37.610 --> 00:37:41.000
inside of the Docker container.

00:37:41.000 --> 00:37:44.060
All we do is simulate
the sensor data.

00:37:44.060 --> 00:37:46.700
And so, what's really
powerful about this

00:37:46.700 --> 00:37:48.200
is that the sensor
data coming in

00:37:48.200 --> 00:37:49.820
is going through
the same pipeline

00:37:49.820 --> 00:37:52.170
that it would go through
in the real world.

00:37:52.170 --> 00:37:54.880
So as we make adjustments,
we can be very confident

00:37:54.880 --> 00:37:56.630
that those adjustments
are going to behave

00:37:56.630 --> 00:38:00.840
the way that we expect
them to in the real world.

00:38:00.840 --> 00:38:03.590
And that really just
gives us that extra bit

00:38:03.590 --> 00:38:07.250
here where things, when
we go to deploy them,

00:38:07.250 --> 00:38:08.500
behave the way that we want.

00:38:11.092 --> 00:38:13.300
So why would we want to test
with hundreds of robots?

00:38:13.300 --> 00:38:15.970
The warehouse layout
change is one thing,

00:38:15.970 --> 00:38:18.220
but another thing is
algorithm changes.

00:38:18.220 --> 00:38:22.030
We have a lot of
logic and algorithms,

00:38:22.030 --> 00:38:24.070
as Chris alluded to
earlier, that batch

00:38:24.070 --> 00:38:28.100
orders and to try to gain more
efficiencies in the warehouse.

00:38:28.100 --> 00:38:30.530
And so, when we change
those algorithms,

00:38:30.530 --> 00:38:34.075
it's not really great for us
to test them out on a customer.

00:38:34.075 --> 00:38:35.700
Because you can
imagine what a customer

00:38:35.700 --> 00:38:37.330
is going to call us and say.

00:38:37.330 --> 00:38:40.090
They're going to call us and
say, hey, Joe, that software

00:38:40.090 --> 00:38:41.200
upgrade you gave me--

00:38:41.200 --> 00:38:43.720
now my rates just fell by 50%.

00:38:43.720 --> 00:38:46.730
And that's not a really
great conversation to have,

00:38:46.730 --> 00:38:49.670
and that's not what we want
to do for our customers.

00:38:49.670 --> 00:38:52.150
And so, with this sort
of infrastructure,

00:38:52.150 --> 00:38:55.150
we can prove algorithm
changes before we deploy them,

00:38:55.150 --> 00:38:57.520
and we can confidently
tell the customer, when

00:38:57.520 --> 00:39:00.370
you take this release, you're
going to get x number of--

00:39:00.370 --> 00:39:01.950
x percent of improvement.

00:39:01.950 --> 00:39:04.030
Your pickers will be
this much more efficient.

00:39:04.030 --> 00:39:06.155
Your warehouse will be able
to get this many orders

00:39:06.155 --> 00:39:08.860
out the door, and be
very confident that's

00:39:08.860 --> 00:39:11.630
an accurate estimate.

00:39:11.630 --> 00:39:14.870
And lastly, and I think-- which
is kind of an interesting way

00:39:14.870 --> 00:39:18.360
to use the system, is that we
have cloud systems as well.

00:39:18.360 --> 00:39:21.110
And we can do synthetic load
testing on those cloud systems

00:39:21.110 --> 00:39:24.920
and throw foobar
data at it, or we

00:39:24.920 --> 00:39:28.490
can run real robots,
real simulated robots,

00:39:28.490 --> 00:39:30.440
against that system
and do load tests

00:39:30.440 --> 00:39:33.410
for warehouses that are
even impractical to build

00:39:33.410 --> 00:39:34.800
in the physical world.

00:39:34.800 --> 00:39:37.670
So we can really put our
other software in the cloud

00:39:37.670 --> 00:39:41.750
through the paces using
a very large deployment

00:39:41.750 --> 00:39:42.650
of simulated Chucks.

00:39:45.240 --> 00:39:49.170
So scaling with Kubernetes
Engine is super easy.

00:39:49.170 --> 00:39:52.050
Auto scaling works
great, and that is really

00:39:52.050 --> 00:39:53.470
important for this problem.

00:39:53.470 --> 00:39:56.130
You don't want to have to
call up a DevOps engineer

00:39:56.130 --> 00:39:59.400
and say, hey, I need a cluster
with 100 simulated Chucks.

00:39:59.400 --> 00:40:00.930
You just want to
tell your software

00:40:00.930 --> 00:40:03.990
I need 100 simulated Chucks,
and then minutes later you

00:40:03.990 --> 00:40:06.660
have 100 simulated Chucks.

00:40:06.660 --> 00:40:09.400
Stackdriver monitoring
is built in.

00:40:09.400 --> 00:40:11.460
And so, you can see
your simulated Chucks

00:40:11.460 --> 00:40:15.360
and see trends in their CPU
usage, their memory usage,

00:40:15.360 --> 00:40:17.820
and see all that and
feed it into BigQuery,

00:40:17.820 --> 00:40:19.620
as Paul alluded to
earlier, and make sure

00:40:19.620 --> 00:40:22.040
that your dashboards perform
the way that they should,

00:40:22.040 --> 00:40:24.457
and that you're seeing the
algorithm improvements that you

00:40:24.457 --> 00:40:25.890
should.

00:40:25.890 --> 00:40:27.960
You can scale up
and down quickly.

00:40:27.960 --> 00:40:29.400
Scaling up is super quick.

00:40:29.400 --> 00:40:31.140
Scaling down is
also super quick,

00:40:31.140 --> 00:40:33.540
because this is an
expensive resource

00:40:33.540 --> 00:40:35.310
and you don't want
to be spending money

00:40:35.310 --> 00:40:38.970
with a simulation cluster that
someone forgot to shut down

00:40:38.970 --> 00:40:42.000
for like a weekend, and
your Google Cloud bill

00:40:42.000 --> 00:40:46.210
all of a sudden is a little
bit higher than you expected.

00:40:46.210 --> 00:40:47.910
So here's a real simulation.

00:40:47.910 --> 00:40:51.480
This is a pretend
warehouse in the cloud

00:40:51.480 --> 00:40:54.690
with pretend robots running
around doing robot things.

00:40:54.690 --> 00:40:57.690
They're picking orders,
they're inducting orders.

00:40:57.690 --> 00:40:59.550
They even go to charge.

00:40:59.550 --> 00:41:01.320
They even have
simulated batteries.

00:41:01.320 --> 00:41:03.870
So because charging
can actually take out

00:41:03.870 --> 00:41:05.700
of the efficiency of the system.

00:41:05.700 --> 00:41:07.750
So we simulate all of that.

00:41:07.750 --> 00:41:11.640
And we can put all the things
I talked about into this.

00:41:11.640 --> 00:41:16.338
And so, this is a very,
very flexible system.

00:41:19.210 --> 00:41:21.900
Another thing that
happens is that when

00:41:21.900 --> 00:41:25.350
you start simulating things,
if you spawn 20 robots

00:41:25.350 --> 00:41:28.440
in the corner of a warehouse,
they kind of form a mosh pit

00:41:28.440 --> 00:41:30.613
and they don't
really do very well.

00:41:30.613 --> 00:41:32.280
And so, these are
some of the fun things

00:41:32.280 --> 00:41:34.920
that you start to discover
when you build a simulation

00:41:34.920 --> 00:41:36.810
platform is like,
oh, wait a second,

00:41:36.810 --> 00:41:39.090
they all can't just
spawn in one place.

00:41:39.090 --> 00:41:42.180
They need to actually
spawn at random points,

00:41:42.180 --> 00:41:43.650
and we need to
kind of figure out

00:41:43.650 --> 00:41:45.310
how we scale this up and down.

00:41:45.310 --> 00:41:48.070
But yeah, that's a fun little
video of a robot mosh pit

00:41:48.070 --> 00:41:49.320
if you ever wanted to see one.

00:41:52.170 --> 00:41:55.590
So the last thing we
need to talk about

00:41:55.590 --> 00:41:57.630
is integrating with
your customers.

00:41:57.630 --> 00:41:59.670
You didn't just build
this robotic IoT

00:41:59.670 --> 00:42:01.050
platform for yourself.

00:42:01.050 --> 00:42:03.383
You built it because you
wanted to bring it to the world

00:42:03.383 --> 00:42:05.910
and you want people to use
it and companies to use it.

00:42:05.910 --> 00:42:08.910
And so, you have
to kind of discover

00:42:08.910 --> 00:42:11.820
who your customers are,
discover what kind of technology

00:42:11.820 --> 00:42:16.010
they have, and you have
to integrate with it.

00:42:16.010 --> 00:42:19.670
So in warehouse
management systems

00:42:19.670 --> 00:42:22.250
are the systems that
handle all the orders,

00:42:22.250 --> 00:42:23.840
handle all the inventory.

00:42:23.840 --> 00:42:27.590
They kind of determine what that
day's picking is going to be,

00:42:27.590 --> 00:42:29.610
and they give us our work.

00:42:29.610 --> 00:42:32.570
And so, we have to
integrate with them.

00:42:32.570 --> 00:42:35.900
Our customers have
older systems.

00:42:35.900 --> 00:42:37.730
They're in the
logistics business.

00:42:37.730 --> 00:42:40.190
They're not in the IT
or the tech business.

00:42:40.190 --> 00:42:42.440
And so, when they find a
solution that works for them,

00:42:42.440 --> 00:42:44.390
they stay with that solution.

00:42:44.390 --> 00:42:47.390
And we don't want to
go into our customers

00:42:47.390 --> 00:42:50.180
and say, well, we wish
we could work with you,

00:42:50.180 --> 00:42:52.460
but you have to update
all of your IT systems

00:42:52.460 --> 00:42:57.120
to adhere to REST
standards into our APIs.

00:42:57.120 --> 00:43:00.140
We have to do
something different.

00:43:00.140 --> 00:43:03.200
So we adapt our
ears our REST APIs

00:43:03.200 --> 00:43:06.450
to our customers' technology.

00:43:06.450 --> 00:43:10.646
They use Socket communication
with fixed-width messages.

00:43:10.646 --> 00:43:14.190
They use SOAP.

00:43:14.190 --> 00:43:15.780
They use SFTP.

00:43:15.780 --> 00:43:17.460
I mean, they use everything.

00:43:17.460 --> 00:43:19.710
And so, that means we
have to build everything

00:43:19.710 --> 00:43:22.240
and we have to adapt
our APIs to everything.

00:43:22.240 --> 00:43:24.510
And so, that's a big
challenge, but luckily,

00:43:24.510 --> 00:43:26.430
when you're using
Google Cloud Platform,

00:43:26.430 --> 00:43:28.740
you have a lot of tools at
your fingertips to help you

00:43:28.740 --> 00:43:30.810
with that challenge.

00:43:30.810 --> 00:43:35.130
We reach for cloud functions for
the simplest of integrations,

00:43:35.130 --> 00:43:38.730
and Kubernetes also makes
super quick with things like

00:43:38.730 --> 00:43:40.980
[? Hey ?] [? Native ?] and
other tools that are coming

00:43:40.980 --> 00:43:43.397
to the Kubernetes platform
where we can build these little

00:43:43.397 --> 00:43:48.240
micro-services that their only
purpose in life is to translate

00:43:48.240 --> 00:43:51.840
our API messages so that our
customers can understand them.

00:43:51.840 --> 00:43:55.793
There's a lot of challenges
still, but with these tools--

00:43:55.793 --> 00:43:57.210
without these tools
we wouldn't be

00:43:57.210 --> 00:43:59.975
able to do what we do today
at the pace and at the scale

00:43:59.975 --> 00:44:00.600
we do it today.

00:44:04.850 --> 00:44:07.880
Google Kubernetes Engine
on-prem is the next great thing

00:44:07.880 --> 00:44:10.640
that 6 River systems
is really looking at.

00:44:10.640 --> 00:44:14.630
And some of our customers
have such tight regulation--

00:44:14.630 --> 00:44:17.450
you can think government
and those type of things,

00:44:17.450 --> 00:44:19.310
where they just
don't have the option

00:44:19.310 --> 00:44:22.250
to have anything outside
of their four walls.

00:44:22.250 --> 00:44:25.370
And so with these
type of deployments,

00:44:25.370 --> 00:44:27.290
we're really excited
to experiment

00:44:27.290 --> 00:44:30.320
and to use Google
Kubernetes on-prem to solve

00:44:30.320 --> 00:44:32.530
these problems.

00:44:32.530 --> 00:44:37.410
So these regulation concerns
are definitely one part of it.

00:44:37.410 --> 00:44:41.510
Another part is bringing
latency sensitive things that

00:44:41.510 --> 00:44:45.530
need to happen to the edge, and
the edge being the customer's

00:44:45.530 --> 00:44:47.640
own infrastructure.

00:44:47.640 --> 00:44:50.660
And so, if we have to validate
something every single time

00:44:50.660 --> 00:44:54.860
we scan it and we can't afford
the trip up to our cloud,

00:44:54.860 --> 00:44:57.920
back to their data
center, back to Chuck,

00:44:57.920 --> 00:45:00.950
then a system like
this makes things

00:45:00.950 --> 00:45:04.400
a lot easier for the
customer and for us

00:45:04.400 --> 00:45:09.140
to get that integration done and
to really have a great customer

00:45:09.140 --> 00:45:11.750
experience.

00:45:11.750 --> 00:45:14.990
From our point of
view, it's so great

00:45:14.990 --> 00:45:18.320
that we can have the same
deployment tooling that

00:45:18.320 --> 00:45:21.110
manages all of our
normal cloud deployments

00:45:21.110 --> 00:45:23.790
manage these on-prem
deployments as well.

00:45:23.790 --> 00:45:29.360
And so, that can reduce our
overhead for DevOps tooling,

00:45:29.360 --> 00:45:31.640
and to keep the
interface that we

00:45:31.640 --> 00:45:35.630
use for our software engineers
and for our tech support

00:45:35.630 --> 00:45:36.830
engineers the same.

00:45:36.830 --> 00:45:40.180
[MUSIC PLAYING]

